From ee34db3f271cea4d4252048617919c2caafe698b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Heiko Carstens <hca@linux.ibm.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Nov 2023 19:37:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] checkstack: fix printed address

All addresses printed by checkstack have an extra incorrect 0 appended at
the end.

This was introduced with commit 677f1410e058 ("scripts/checkstack.pl: don't
display $dre as different entity"): since then the address is taken from
the line which contains the function name, instead of the line which
contains stack consumption. E.g. on s390:

0000000000100a30 <do_one_initcall>:
...
  100a44:       e3 f0 ff 70 ff 71       lay     %r15,-144(%r15)

So the used regex which matches spaces and hexadecimal numbers to extract
an address now matches a different substring. Subsequently replacing spaces
with 0 appends a zero at the and, instead of replacing leading spaces.

Fix this by using the proper regex, and simplify the code a bit.

Link: https://lkml.kernel.org/r/20231120183719.2188479-2-hca@linux.ibm.com
Fixes: 677f1410e058 ("scripts/checkstack.pl: don't display $dre as different entity")
Signed-off-by: Heiko Carstens <hca@linux.ibm.com>
Cc: Maninder Singh <maninder1.s@samsung.com>
Cc: Masahiro Yamada <masahiroy@kernel.org>
Cc: Vaneet Narang <v.narang@samsung.com>
Cc: <stable@vger.kernel.org>
Signed-off-by: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>

diff --git a/scripts/checkstack.pl b/scripts/checkstack.pl
index 84f5fb7f1cec..ac74f8629cea 100755
--- a/scripts/checkstack.pl
+++ b/scripts/checkstack.pl
@@ -139,15 +139,11 @@ $total_size = 0;
 while (my $line = <STDIN>) {
 	if ($line =~ m/$funcre/) {
 		$func = $1;
-		next if $line !~ m/^($xs*)/;
+		next if $line !~ m/^($x*)/;
 		if ($total_size > $min_stack) {
 			push @stack, "$intro$total_size\n";
 		}
-
-		$addr = $1;
-		$addr =~ s/ /0/g;
-		$addr = "0x$addr";
-
+		$addr = "0x$1";
 		$intro = "$addr $func [$file]:";
 		my $padlen = 56 - length($intro);
 		while ($padlen > 0) {
-- 
2.25.1

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```perl
next if $line !~ m/^($xs*)/;
$addr =~ s/ /0/g;
$addr = "0x$addr";
```
Reason:
The first statement uses an incorrect regex pattern (`$xs*`) that does not properly match the intended address format, leading to the wrong substring being captured. The second statement replaces spaces with '0', which appends an extra '0' to the address instead of correctly formatting it. The third statement constructs the address string with the incorrect value, resulting in the printed address having an extra '0' at the end.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```perl
next if $line !~ m/^($x*)/;
$addr = "0x$1";
```
Reason:
The first statement is updated to use the correct regex pattern (`$x*`), which accurately matches the intended address format. The second statement directly assigns the captured address (without unnecessary space replacement) to the `$addr` variable, ensuring that the address is correctly formatted as a hexadecimal string without any appended '0'.

